The European Muon Collaboration (EMC) conducted an experiment which measured the contribution of quark and anti-quark spins to the proton spin [1, 2]. The experiment consisted of deep inelastic scattering (DIS) of longitudinally polarized muons off a fixed target that contained longitudinally polarized protons. The DIS cross section can be written as a linear combination of the polarized structure functions \(g_{1}(x,Q^{2})\) and \(g_{2}(x,Q^{2})\) of the nucleon,

\[\frac{d^{2}\sigma^{++}}{dQ^{2}dx}-\frac{d^{2}\sigma^{+-}}{dQ^{2} dx} =\] \[\frac{16\pi\alpha^{2}}{Q^{4}}\left[g_{1}(x,Q^{2})y\left(\frac{y}{ 2}-1+\frac{Mxy}{E}\right)+g_{2}(x,Q^{2})\frac{Mx}{E}\right],\]

where the superscripts \(+(-)\) denote the lepton and nucleon helicity states, and the lepton mass and parity-violating terms are neglected. The other variables are

\[M : \mbox{mass of the struck nucleon},\] \[E : \mbox{initial lepton energy},\] \[x : \mbox{Bjorken scaling variable},\] \[y : \mbox{photon virtuality}.\]

In experiments with high beam energies, the effects from \(g_{2}(x,Q^{2})\) are kinematically suppressed and can typically be neglected. The parity-conserving unpolarized DIS cross section can be similarly expressed in terms of structure functions \(F_{1}(x,Q^{2})\) and \(F_{2}(x,Q^{2})\)[10]. The unpolarized and polarized structure func

Figure 1.1: (a) Unpolarized and (b) polarized parton distribution functions.

tions have intuitive leading-order interpretations in terms of the parton distribution functions,

\[F_{1}\left(x\right) =\sum_{q}e_{q}^{2}q\left(x\right), \tag{1.5}\] \[F_{2}\left(x\right) =2x\sum_{q}e_{q}^{2}q\left(x\right),\] (1.6) \[g_{1}\left(x\right) =\sum_{q}e_{q}^{2}\Delta q\left(x\right),\] (1.7) \[g_{2}\left(x\right) =0, \tag{1.8}\]

where \(e_{q}\) denotes the quark charge and \(q(x)\) and \(\Delta q(x)\) are the unpolarized and polarized quark distribution functions. Gluons do not enter in these leading order expressions, since the DIS photon does not couple directly to gluons. The leading order expressions furthermore show the well-known Bjorken scaling behavior, the (leading order) independence of the structure functions on \(Q^{2}\), and the Callan-Gross relation \(2xF_{1}(x)=F_{2}(x)\)[10, 11]. Beyond leading order, the structure functions acquire a dependence in \(Q^{2}\) that is logarithmic.

The first moment of \(g_{1}^{p}(x,Q^{2})\) can be expressed using the linear combination of \(a_{3}\), \(a_{8}\), and \(a_{0}(Q^{2})\),

\[\Gamma_{1}^{p}\left(Q^{2}\right) = \int_{0}^{1}g_{1}^{p}\left(x,Q^{2}\right)dx\] \[= \frac{1}{12}\left[\left(1-\frac{\alpha_{s}(Q^{2})}{\pi}\right) \left(a_{3}+\frac{a_{8}}{\sqrt{3}}\right)\right.\] \[\left.+\sqrt{\frac{8}{3}}\left(1-\frac{33-8n_{f}}{33-2n_{f}} \frac{\alpha_{s}(Q^{2})}{\pi}\right)a_{0}(Q^{2})\right]\!,\]

where \(a_{3}\), \(a_{8}\), and \(a_{0}\) are the proton matrix elements of the axial vector currents, \(A_{5\mu}=\bar{\psi}\gamma_{u}\gamma_{5}\psi\), \(n_{f}\) is the number of active quark flavors, and \(\alpha_{s}\) is the strong coupling constant [10]. Terms of \(\mathcal{O}(\alpha_{s}^{2})\) are neglected. At leading order, the matrix elements are related to the helicity quark distributions as follows:

\[a_{3} =\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}\,\big{[}(\Delta u+\Delta\overline{u})-(\Delta d +\Delta\overline{d})\big{]}, \tag{1.10}\] \[a_{8} =(\Delta u+\Delta\overline{u})+(\Delta d+\Delta\overline{d})-2\,( \Delta s+\Delta\overline{s}),\] \[a_{0} =\sqrt{\frac{1}{3}}\,\big{[}(\Delta u+\Delta\overline{u})+( \Delta d+\Delta\overline{d})+(\Delta s+\Delta\overline{s})\big{]}.\]

Experimental results from polarized neutron decay [14] and hyperon beta decay [15] are used to determine \(a_{3}\) and \(a_{8}\) respectively [16, 17]. The quark spin contribution to the proton spin, \(\Delta\Sigma\), is then extracted from the measured value of \(\Gamma_{1}^{p}\), using the values of \(a_{3}\) and \(a_{8}\) from neutron and hyperon leptonic decay. The first moment of \(g_{1}^{p}\) is extrapolated for \(x<0.01\) and \(x>0.7\) to cover \(0<x<1\). The EMC obtained \(\Gamma_{1}^{p}=0.126\pm 0.010_{\rm stat.}\pm 0.015_{\rm syst.}\), well below the value of \(0.192\pm 0.002\) anticipated from the Ellis-Jaffe sum rule [18]. The Ellis-Jaffe sum rule assumes \(\Delta s=\Delta\overline{s}=0\) and SU(3) flavor symmetry. Conversely, the quark and anti-quark spin contributions for the individual quark flavors can be obtained from the measured moment, the hyperon leptonic decay couplings, and the relations from Equation 1.10. In this way, only \((12\pm 9\pm 14)\%\) of the proton spin is found to arise from the spin of its constituent quarks. The strange quark spin contribution is found to be \(\frac{1}{2}(\Delta s+\Delta\overline{s})=-0.095\)\(\pm\)\(0.016_{\rm stat.}\)\(\pm\)\(0.023_{\rm syst.}\), hence the strange quarks are found polarized anti-parallel to the proton spin. The above EMC results and implications, as well as the implication that \(g_{1}^{n}\) is negative, have motivated substantial follow-up research.

Other DIS experiments have been performed, extending the \(x\) range coverage. Targets with polarized neutrons and deuterons have been used in addition to targets with polarized protons which have allowed to confirm the validity of the more fundamental spin sum rule by Bjorken [1]. Figure 1.2 shows a recent compilation of results from several experiments for the polarized structure function of both the proton and deuteron [19]. The experimental results confirm the EMCdata with better precision and larger \(x\) coverage, and therefore confirm that the quark spin contribution to the proton spin is surprisingly small.

In addition to the above inclusive polarized DIS experiments, polarized semi-inclusive DIS (SIDIS) experiments have been performed. In SIDIS experiments, a hadron is observed in the final state in addition to the scattered lepton. Such experiments, in particular those with identified hadrons, make it possible to more selectively study individual quark and anti-quark spin contributions to the proton spin. For example, the HERMES experiment has obtained SIDIS data with identified charged kaons in positron scattering off a polarized deuterium target [1]. The double spin asymmetry of the charged kaon production was used, together with fragmentation functions, in a leading-order analysis to determine (\(\Delta s\) + \(\Delta\overline{s}\)) for 0.023 \(<\)\(x\) \(<\) 0.06 at \(Q^{2}\)=2.5 GeV\({}^{2}\). Figure 1.3 shows the results for \(\Delta Q\) = \(\Delta u\) + \(\Delta\overline{u}\) + \(\Delta d\) + \(\Delta\overline{d}\) and \(\Delta S\) = \(\Delta s\) + \(\Delta\overline{s}\). The obtained strange quark helicity distri

