#### 3.1.2 Selection Parameter Tuning to Further Reduce Combinatorial Background

The background in the initial \(\rm V^{0}\) candidate yields can be further reduced by tuning the selection cut parameters. For example, several of the topological and kinematic parameters are expected to vary with transverse momentum \(\rm p_{T}\). By separating the \(\rm V^{0}\) particles for different \(\rm p_{T}\) intervals, the selection cuts can be tuned for each \(\rm p_{T}\) interval.

The signal and background region in the invariant mass spectrum are first defined for each \(\rm V^{0}\) species prior to tuning the topological and kinematic parameter values. The background region for the three \(\rm V^{0}\) species is defined by two fixed sideband ranges in the invariant mass spectrum on either side of the nominal mass for all \(\rm p_{T}\) intervals. The signal region is determined for each \(\rm p_{T}\) interval by fitting a Lorentzian function to the mass spectrum. The signal range is defined as 1.75 times the full width half maximum (FWHM) centered at the fitted pole mass value for each \(\rm p_{T}\) interval. Table 3.2 shows the signal and background range values for all \(\rm V^{0}\) species for run year 2006 and 2009.

The combinatorial background in the initial particle samples are further reduced prior to tuning the selection cut parameters. A minimum threshold of 0.2 rad is imposed to the opening angle of the decay daughter tracks with \(n\sigma_{e}>-2\) to reduce electron contamination. By identifying \(\rm V^{0}\) candidates with an improbable decay length, one can further remove backgrounds from the particle yields [16]. The decay length probability, \(P_{d}\), for \(\rm V^{0}\) is defined as

\[P_{d}=1-e^{-\frac{M_{V^{0}}\cdot d}{|\bf{pv^{0}}|\cdot c\cdot\tau}}, \tag{3.8}\]

where

