#### Hyperon-Jet Association

Run year 2006 and 2009 used trigger conditions to record events with high \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) jets. The association of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s to triggered jets increases the likelihood that the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s originated from hard 2\(\rightarrow\)2 scattering collisions. The associated \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s are categorized as near-side and away-side \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s as shown in Figure 3.37. Near-side \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s are defined as \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s residing within the jet cone radius of 0.7,

\[R=\sqrt{(\Delta\eta)^{2}+(\Delta\phi)^{2}}=0.7, \tag{3.35}\]

from a triggered jet as shown in Figures 3.38(a)-(f) while \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s residing between \(\Delta\phi\)[1.7, 4.6] rad on the transverse plane from a triggered jet are defined as away-side \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s. The \(\Delta\phi\) range is chosen so as to discriminate signal from underlying event background as seen in Figures 3.39(a)-(f). In categorizing the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s,

Figure 3.37: Diagram of hyperon-jet association. Near-side \(\Lambda\)s reside within the triggered jet cone radius while away-side \(\Lambda\)s reside within the \(\phi\) range away from the triggered jet.

the near-side takes priority if the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) association satisfies both near-side and away-side conditions for events with multiple trigger jets. The \(\mathrm{K^{0}_{s}}\) particles were associated to jets and categorized in the same way.

Jets are comprised of a collection of particles, each contributing to the jet's total momentum. The momentum fraction, \(z\), can be calculated for each constituent particle as

\[z=\frac{\mathbf{p}_{\mathrm{f}}\cdot\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{j}}}{P^{2}}, \tag{3.36}\]

where \(\mathbf{p}_{\mathrm{f}}\) and \(\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{j}}\) are the momentum for the constituent particle and jet, respectively. Using the \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) component for the associated \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) and triggered jet, the jet \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) fragmentation distribution for different \(\mathrm{p_{T}^{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}}\) intervals are shown in Figures 3.40-3.42.

There is a positive trend of the average \(z\) values with \(\mathrm{p_{T}^{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}}\), as expected. With increasing \(\mathrm{p_{T}^{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}}\) the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s are increasingly likely to be the leading particle in the jet, contributing most of the jet momentum. In calculating \(z\), no corrections were performed for detector acceptance, resolution, or efficiency on the jet energy scale. Table 3.21 shows the hyperon-jet associated yields of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) and \(\mathrm{K^{0}_{s}}\) particles within the invariant mass signal region for run year 2006 and 2009. The residual background contribution was recalculated for the hyperon-jet associated yields which are summarized in Table 3.22. There is an overall increase of the residual background contribution for all trigger sets after imposing hyperon-jet association. The highest \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) interval contains the largest residual background contribution for all particle species and trigger sets. This is expected since the observed invariant mass peak widens as a \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) increases because of resolution effects, thus increasing the underlying background counts. Figures 3.43(a)-(f) show the \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) distributions of near-side and away-side \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s which reside within the invariant mass signal region for run year 2006 and 2009 triggers. The near-side \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s contain higher \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\)\(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s compared to the away-side sample for both run years.

Jets are reconstructed using TPC charged tracks and BEMC neutral energy deposits. The reconstruction therefore does not explicitly consider the hyperons of interest. Although a reconstructed hyperon resides within the triggered jet cone after association, the decay daughters may not. This is expected to happen more to the \(\pi^{+}(\pi^{-})\) daughters, which tend to carry a smaller portion of the hyperon's momentum and be produced at larger angles. Furthermore, the jet reconstruction uses only tracks classified as primary tracks. Primary tracks are particle tracks that point back to the primary vertex and are used to determine to the primary vertex. The hyperon decay daughter tracks originate from a secondary vertex, and may not be classified as primary tracks. Figures 3.44-3.45 show the number distributions when neither, either, or both of the decay daughters are part of the jet finding and reconstruction for \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) hyperons associated with jets from jet triggered data for run year 2006 and 2009 (continuous lines). For each of the four entries in these distributions, the colored entries indicate if the decay daughters were primary or global tracks. Most of the daughters are identified as global tracks when neither of the daughters contribute to the associated jet's reconstruction as expected. The \(p(\overline{p})\) from \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) hyperon decay contributed 70-79% to the associated jet's reconstruction for run year 2006 and 2009. This is expected as \(p(\overline{p})\) carries more of the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)'s momentum. Daughter particles identified as primary tracks contributed 72-79% to the associated jet's reconstruction for run year 2006 and 2009.

