The \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) for \(\Lambda\) is extracted using

\[D_{LL}^{\Lambda}=\frac{1}{P_{beam}\alpha_{w}\langle\cos\left(\theta^{*} \right)\rangle}\frac{N_{\Lambda}^{+}-N_{\Lambda}^{-}}{N_{\Lambda}^{+}+N_{ \Lambda}^{-}}, \tag{3.47}\]

where \(N_{\Lambda}^{+(-)}\) define the helicity sorted luminosity corrected \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) counts. The superscript \(+(-)\) denotes the helicity state. Both proton beams are longitudinally polarized in the recorded events. Samples with different spin states are combined to spin average one of the polarized proton beams. A separate \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) measurement can be made for each polarized beam. The raw spin sorted \(\Lambda\) counts are luminosity corrected using the relative luminosity ratios to compensate for different beam intensities at each spin bit state. The longitudinally polarized blue beam \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) can be extracted using

\[D_{LL,B}^{\Lambda}=\frac{1}{P_{B}\alpha_{w}\langle\cos\left(\theta^{*}\right) \rangle}\frac{\left(\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{-+}} {R_{6}}\right)-\left(n_{\Lambda,B}^{--}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)} {\left(\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{-+}}{R_{6}} \right)+\left(n_{\Lambda,B}^{--}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,B}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)} \tag{3.48}\]

and the longitudinally polarized yellow beam using

\[D_{LL,Y}^{\Lambda}=\frac{1}{P_{Y}\alpha_{w}\langle\cos\left(\theta^{*}\right) \rangle}\frac{\left(\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{+-} }{R_{5}}\right)-\left(n_{\Lambda,Y}^{--}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{-+}}{R_{6}} \right)}{\left(\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{+-}}{R_{5 }}\right)+\left(n_{\Lambda,Y}^{--}+\frac{n_{\Lambda,Y}^{-+}}{R_{6}}\right)}, \tag{3.49}\]

where

\[R_{4}\,,\;R_{5}\,,\;R_{6} : \text{Relative luminosity ratios for polarized}\] \[\text{proton-proton spin states},\] \[n_{\Lambda,B(Y)}^{++}\,,\;n_{\Lambda,B(Y)}^{-+}\,,\] \[n_{\Lambda,B(Y)}^{+-}\,,\;n_{\Lambda,B(Y)}^{--}\]

The relative luminosities, \(\mathrm{R_{1\text{-}6}}\), were previously defined in Section 2.2.6. Using detector symmetry relations shown in Figure 3.47 and the parity conservation relations from Equation 3.43, the blue and yellow longitudinally polarized beam \(\Lambda\) counts can be merged to extract one \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) measurement in the forward, \(\eta_{physics}>0\), and one in the backward, \(\eta_{physics}<0\), direction. This increases the statistics used for the extracted \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) measurements. For the forward direction, the \(\mathrm{D_{LL}}\) measure ment for \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) can be extracted using

\[D_{LL} = \frac{1}{\overline{P}_{beam}\alpha_{w}\langle\cos{(\theta^{*})} \rangle}\] \[\times\frac{\left(\frac{n_{B}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{B}^{-+}}{R_{6}} \right)+\left(\frac{n_{Y}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{Y}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)-\left(n _{B}^{--}+\frac{n_{B}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)-\left(n_{Y}^{--}+\frac{n_{Y}^{-+}}{R _{6}}\right)}{\left(\frac{n_{B}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{B}^{-+}}{R_{6}}\right)+ \left(\frac{n_{Y}^{++}}{R_{4}}+\frac{n_{Y}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)+\left(n_{B}^{-- }+\frac{n_{B}^{+-}}{R_{5}}\right)+\left(n_{Y}^{--}+\frac{n_{Y}^{-+}}{R_{6}} \right)},\]

where \(\overline{P}_{beam}\) is the mean polarization value for the blue and yellow beam.

The D\({}_{\rm LL}\) measurement is performed in equally spaced \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) intervals for the \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) range of [\(-\)1,1]. Twenty \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) intervals are used to cancel the acceptance function in the extracted D\({}_{\rm LL}\). For each \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) interval, the D\({}_{\rm LL}\) is extracted using Equation 3.50. The weak decay parameter value from PDG [1],\(\alpha_{\rm w}=0.642(-0.642)\)\(\pm\) 0.013, was used for \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\)s. For the \(\rm K^{0}_{s}\) sample, an artificial weak parameter, \(\alpha^{{}^{\prime}}_{\rm w}=1\), was used when extracting the \(\rm D_{LL}\). The mean \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) value is calculated using the relation,

\[\langle\cos{(\theta^{*})}\rangle=\frac{\sum_{i}^{N}\cos{(\theta^{*})}}{N}, \tag{3.51}\]

where the \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) values are obtained from all the spin sorted counts, \(N\), extracted from all fills. The polarization values for each beam have been calculated for each fill. The duration of each fill varies and the trigger conditions may vary for each run, making the effective data recording time different per fill. Using the number of events which satisfy the trigger conditions of interest for each fill, an overall weighted mean beam polarization was calculated for all fills, \(F\), using,

\[P_{beam}=\frac{\sum_{j}^{F}m_{j}\overline{P}_{j}}{\sum_{j}^{F}m_ {j}}, \tag{3.52}\]

where \(m_{j}\) is the number of events satisfying the trigger conditions in each fill. The beam polarization value per fill, \(\overline{P}_{j}\), is the mean between the blue and yellow beam polarizations with no additional weights because the number of events recorded is the same for both blue and yellow beams. The spin sorted \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) yields counts for all runs, \(G\), are defined as

\[N^{+} =\sum_{k}^{G}\left[N_{B,k}^{+}+N_{Y,k}^{+}\right] \tag{3.53}\] \[=\sum_{B,k}^{G}\left[\left(\frac{n_{B,k}^{++}}{R_{4,k}}+\frac{n_ {B,k}^{-+}}{R_{6,k}}\right)+\left(\frac{n_{Y,k}^{++}}{R_{4,k}}+\frac{n_{Y,k}^{ +-}}{R_{5,k}}\right)\right]\]\[N^{-} = \sum_{k}^{G}\left[N_{B,k}^{-}+N_{Y,k}^{-}\right]\] \[= \sum_{B,k}^{G}\Bigg{[}\Bigg{(}n_{B,k}^{--}+\frac{n_{B,k}^{+-}}{R_{5,k}}\Bigg{)}+\Bigg{(}n_{Y,k}^{--}+\frac{n_{Y,k}^{-+}}{R_{6,k}}\Bigg{)}\Bigg{]}.\]

The relative beam luminosity values are used to correct the difference between the beam intensities. The mean D\({}_{\rm LL}\) value between all \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) intervals are calculated. Near-side D\({}_{\rm LL}\) values within \(2.0\leq\rm{p_{T}}<3.0\;\rm{GeV}\) and \(\eta_{physics}>0\) are shown for \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) and K\({}_{\rm s}^{0}\) particles which reside in the signal range in Figures 3.48(a)-(c). The \(\langle\cos{(\theta^{*})}\rangle\) value in the denominator increases the D\({}_{\rm LL}\) values and their uncertainties for \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) intervals near \(\cos{(\theta^{*})}\) = 0.

The extracted D\({}_{\rm LL}\) values residing within the particle candidate invariant mass range still contain a residual background contribution. The background consists of combinatorial background and K\({}_{\rm s}^{0}\) contamination. They are expected to be comprised of mostly unpolarized particles and their primary effect is thus to dilute the extracted D\({}_{\rm LL}\). A background D\({}_{\rm LL}^{\rm Bkg}\) is extracted using the particles which reside within the invariant mass range for background. The background D\({}_{\rm LL}\) measurement is subtracted as

\[D_{LL}^{physics}=\frac{D_{LL}^{Sig}-rD_{LL}^{Bkg}}{1-r}, \tag{3.55}\]

where \(r\) is the average fractional residual background contained within the signal range. The corrected D\({}_{\rm LL}\) is defined as D\({}_{\rm LL}^{\rm physics}\) and has a statistical uncertainty of

\[\delta D_{LL}^{physics}=\frac{\sqrt{\left(\delta D_{LL}^{Sig}\right)^{2}+ \left(r\delta D_{LL}^{Bkg}\right)^{2}}}{1-r}. \tag{3.56}\]

The residual backgrounds for JP1-6 and JP1-9(L2JH-9) are 1.7% and 0.3(1.0)%, respectively, and therefore increase the D\({}_{\rm LL}\) uncertainty by more than 1.8% and \(0.3(1.0)\%\). In run year 2005, the \(\rm D_{LL}\) uncertainty increases because of background was more than 10% and the average residual background value was 11%.

